  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA relatively quiet morning across the area with low stratus\nbanked up against the eastern slopes of the mountains courtesy\nof southeast flow, while mainly clear skies on the lee side of \nthe higher terrain. Low stratus is once again resulting in \nreduced visibility across the higher terrain, with a Special \nWeather Statement currently out across portions of the forecast \narea to account for this. Elsewhere, stout H925 flow of 25-30\nkts has prevented fog development despite the recent wet weather.\nFurther northwest, ISOLD showers continue across the Mid-Ohio \nValley amid lingering low-level surface convergence. This \nactivity is light for the most part, with no flooding concerns \nexpected with it.\n\nShower and isolated thunderstorm activity is expected to\nsteadily increase throughout the day courtesy of forcing from \nan approaching shortwave amid modest diurnal destabilization \n(Mixed-layer CAPE ~ 500-750 J/kg). While severe weather is not \nexpected, locally heavy rainfall is once again a concern given \nprogged PWATs in excess of 1.7\", combined with antecedent soils \nthat are highly compromised by prior rainfall the past few days,\nespecially across the Mid-Ohio Valley. Given such, WPC has \noutlooked all of the forecast area under either a Marginal or \nSlight Risk for excessive rainfall today. While most CAMs do \ndepict corridors of 1-2\"+ of rainfall with activity this \nafternoon into early tonight, there is significant uncertainty \nas to where the heaviest rain corridors will be, leading to \nlower confidence at this point in regards to a Flood Watch for \nflash flooding. This will be monitored going forward, with the \npotential for a Flood Watch if confidence increases. FFG is \nquite low across the northern CWA, while much higher across our \nsouthern zones. Highs today will be slightly above normal, with \nupper 70s to low 80s across the lowlands.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA cold front will attempt to cross through the forecast area \nduring the Monday to Thursday timeframe, with guidance having \nthe front positioned across the southern CWA on Monday, then \neventually lifting back north by midweek, before finally sliding\nsouthwest of the area late Thursday into Friday as a trough\nacross the northeast retrogrades. This will lead to a continued\nunsettled pattern, with likely PoPs progged each day in the \nvicinity of the front, enhanced by diurnal heating. Any severe \npotential will remain on the low side, but flooding concerns \nwill linger, with WPC having a Marginal Risk for excessive \nrainfall each day for at least a portion of the forecast area. \nDrier and cooler conditions build into the region beginning \nFriday and into next weekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nTemperatures hover above normal through the Holiday weekend and\ninto the upcoming short work week, especially on lows, with \nhighs somewhat muffled amid the active weather pattern. \nTemperatures then trend back down to normal or slightly below  \nat the end of the work week and into next weekend.",
